In article <4915@aplcen.apl.jhu.edu> theune@aplcen.apl.jhu.edu (Peter Theune) writes:
>swarren@convex.com (Steve Warren) writes:
>
>>OK, then why not loose the "pay" command, and instead replace it with
>>"settle-up".  When you drop things the shopkeeper says:
>> 
>>           "I'll give 37 for that worthless rusted longsword."
>
>>When you pick things up he tells you the charge for them as usual.  When
>>you settle-up everything is paid both ways at the same time.  This would
>>also allow you to drop items several times, trying to get the best price
>>for them.
>
>
>Yea, sorta like MORIA ,right?

Well, not exactly, since Moria *insists* on bargaining, unless you only
want to get peanuts for your items.  This wouldn't enforce bargaining,
it would just let you try for a little better deal if you want.  The
main thing is there are times when you *have* to drop things in a shop,
even though you don't really want to sell them.  Like when you are
surrounded by enemies and suddenly you loose some strength, and you
can't fight without collapsing.  It would be nice to be able to drop
most of your stuff without having to buy it all back after the fight.

BTW, I think the settle-up command would be the best way to implement
it (just include sell intrinsically with "p").  This way you don't
have to add a new command.  It is simple to make sure you haven't
dropped anything you don't want to sell before paying.  If you can't
carry both items then you ought to sell something anyway.

